      "content": "Research consistently shows that media coverage of racialized protests often employs radically different vocabulary and framing compared to property-oriented demonstrations. Reports on suburban tax protests typically use neutral or positive modifiers like \"community organization\" and focus on policy outcomes, whereas coverage of minority-led protests against police often relies on loaded terms like \"riots,\" \"lawless,\" or \"unrest,\" even when the actions are identical. This linguistic divergence creates a stark contrast in overall sentiment, portraying one event as a lawful civic duty and the other as a chaotic cyclical threat to public order. Such framing bias is a well-documented phenomenon in political science that significantly shapes public perception depending on the race of the protest authors and the location involved.",
